---
id: org.dxos.spec.qa
name: DSL.QA
version: 1.1
extends:
  - org.dxos.spec.core
---

The QA dialect. Adds the executable half of a specification, so a `.mdl` document doubles as a
test plan that a human tester and an agent tester execute from the same source.

The vocabulary is borrowed, not invented, and comes in two layers:

| Layer | Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Gherkin — criteria, derivable | `feat` / `req` | a feature and its requirements |
| | `scenario T-n` | given / when / then; a criterion, not a script (Deus.Std) |
| xUnit — execution, runnable | `suite` | a named set of tests, selected by name or tag |
| | `test QA-n` | one executable case: `before` / `steps` / `after` |
| | `step` | one move: a human instruction and, usually, one operation |

A `test` is an ordered list of steps with a success criterion per step. Each step carries two
faces: a human instruction and, where the step maps to an invocable operation, an agent binding.
Neither face is a rendering of the other — a human clicks a navtree row, an agent invokes an
operation, and both are the truth about how that actor performs the step.

Tests live in a `## QA` section of a `PLUGIN.mdl`, or in `spec/APP.mdl` of an application for
journeys that cross plugins. Suites live beside the tests they group.

## Extensions

| Term    | URI                        |
| ------- | -------------------------- |
| `test`  | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`    |
| `step`  | `org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0`    |
| `suite` | `org.dxos.mdl.suite@1.0`   |
| `op`    | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`      |

`org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0` named the given/when/then block until 2026-09-08; that block is now
`scenario` (`org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`) and `test` is the executable one. A document declaring
`test` in its Extensions table means this definition.

---

## Relationship to `scenario`

`scenario` and `test` are not competitors and must not be collapsed:

| | `scenario T-n` | `test QA-n` |
|---|---|---|
| Kind | derivable criterion | orchestrable script |
| Shape | given / when / then | N ordered steps in three stages |
| Target | vitest, storybook play | the running app |
| Asserted by | the test runner | the human, or an agent through the debug port |

A test's `covers:` names the `feat` / `req` / `scenario` identifiers it exercises, so coverage is
a question the linter can answer rather than a guess.

---

## The `test` Block

```mdl
ext test
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0
  desc: |
    An end-to-end case against the running application, executed by a human tester or an
    agent. Three stages of ordered steps, each step with its own success criterion.
  fields:
    desc?:     Prose
    actors?:   human | agent | both     # default both; see the invoke rule below
    covers?:   RefList                  # feat / req / scenario ids this test exercises
    status?:   passing | failing | blocked | unverified   # last known result, with a date in a comment
    given?:    GivenList                # preconditions; `name: prose` binds `$given.name` (see below)
    before?:   StepList                 # fixture the test needs, but is not itself testing
    steps:     StepList                 # the test — the steps whose failure is a defect
    after?:    StepList                 # returning the app to its pre-test state
                                        # all three are arrays of the same `step` shape
    then?:     ProseList                # whole-test criteria, beyond the per-step ones
    tags?:     TagList
  nesting: none
```

```mdl
ext step
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0
  desc: |
    One move in a test — an item of a stage list, not a block of its own. `do` and `expect`
    are required: that is what keeps a test human-runnable, and a step no human can perform is a
    design smell rather than a shortcut. A step invokes at most one operation.
  fields:
    name?:     Prose          # short title, for the report table
    id?:       Identifier     # stable handle; see the numbering rule below
    do:        Prose          # the human instruction (required)
    expect:    Prose          # the success criterion BOTH actors judge (required)
    invoke?:   OpInvocation   # operation key + input literal; one operation per step
    space?:    Expr           # the space the invoker receives; default `$given.space`, else the default space
    capture?:  Identifier     # names this step's result for later steps as `$<name>`
    assert?:   CodeBlock      # deterministic check evaluated in the debug port; must return truthy
    note?:     Prose          # a constraint the runner must respect (see Execution Rules)
    blocked?:  Prose          # why this step cannot run at all, and what must change first
```

Steps are items of a list, not repeated `step <n>:` keys. Core declares block bodies as
`key[?]: value`, with `key:` followed by an indented `-` list as the multi-line form — which
`given` already uses. A pseudo-key carrying a positional id is not core syntax, and
it forces every later step to be renumbered by hand when one is inserted.

### Field rules

1. **`do` and `expect` are required on every step.** A test whose steps only an agent can perform
   is not a QA plan, it is a script.
2. **`invoke` is optional, and its absence is meaningful.** A step with no operation behind it —
   a drag, a page reload, a judgement about flicker — is a first-class step. A test containing any
   such step cannot declare `actors: agent`.
3. **`assert` is optional.** `expect` prose is always the criterion; `assert` only makes it
   deterministic where that is cheap. Requiring it would make authoring a test a coding task.
4. **`capture` threads a result forward** as `$<name>`, referenced in a later step's `invoke`.
   Within a step, `$result` is that step's own `invoke` result, for use in its `assert`, and
   `$<name>.snapshot` is the snapshot taken after that step, for an operation that returns less
   than the step produced. Only serializable values cross a snippet boundary — an id does, a live
   ECHO object does not (Rule 2).
5. **`$snapshot` is the app's state after the step.** The runner takes one
   `org.dxos.operation.debug.snapshot` after every step's `invoke` (or, for a step with none,
   after the human reports it done) and binds it as `$snapshot` for the step's `assert`. It
   carries `layout`, `planks`, `spaces`, `toasts`, `surfaces`, `attention`, `plugins`, and the
   `errors` logged since the run started. A step judges the app by its snapshot or by a query
   operation, never by the `invoke` return value (Rule 3). When a test needs something the snapshot
   does not report, extend the snapshot operation rather than scripting the page.
6. **A `given` entry written `name: prose` binds `$given.name`.** A bare entry is a condition to
   check; a named one is also a value the runner must resolve and hold for the steps to reference
   (`$given.collection`). Without this, a step's `invoke` would name something the test never
   defined.
7. **Steps are numbered by position**, from 1 within their stage, so `QA-1.3` is the third item
   of `steps`. A step that needs a handle stable across insertions declares `id:`, and is then
   referenced by it (`QA-1.open`) instead. Most steps do not need one; give an `id` to a step
   whose failure is cited somewhere outside the document.

### Input literals

An `invoke` is an operation key followed by a JSON-shaped input. Inside it:

| Form | Meaning | Snippet the runner emits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `$name`, `$name.field`, `$name.snapshot.…` | a captured result or a `given` binding; any JS path over it | the held value |
| `$runId` | this run's id, so artifacts are attributable | the string |
| `Obj("echo://S/O")` | the live object at that URI | `dxos.spaces('S').db.getObjectById('O')` |
| `Ref("echo://S/O")` | a reference to it, for `Ref` inputs | `dxos.Ref.make(…getObjectById('O'))` |
| `Space("S")` | the live space, for `space` inputs | `dxos.spaces('S')` |

Placeholders may appear inside strings (`"QA: Basics $runId"`, `"root/$given.space.id/…"`). A
`create` operation returns a URI, not an object, precisely so that a later step can name the
object through `Obj(…)` after a snippet boundary. A held value that is an `echo://` URI also
exposes `.spaceId` and `.objectId`, parsed by the runner, so a navigation path is written
`"root/$space.id/content/collections/$doc.objectId"` without a step to split the URI.

### Operation references

Within the document, an operation declared here is referenced as `[op:<id>]`, which resolves via
that block's `key:` field (`op@1.1`).

An operation owned by **another** plugin is written as a full key literal. This is not only an
`APP.mdl` concern: a plugin-scoped test routinely drives `space.addObject` and `appToolkit.open`,
and neither is declared in the plugin's own spec.

---

## The `suite` Block

```mdl
ext suite
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.suite@1.0
  desc: |
    A named set of tests, run together and reported together. A container by reference: the
    tests stay standalone blocks, and a suite lists them by id — `QA-1` for a test in the same
    document, `markdown:QA-1` for another plugin's (`app:QA-1` for the application spec).
  fields:
    desc?:   Prose
    tags?:   TagList        # `run --tag nightly` selects every suite carrying the tag
    tests:   RefList        # ordered for the report; execution is order-independent
  nesting: none
```

1. **Suites are order-independent by construction.** Every test owns its fixture (`given` +
   `before`) and its teardown (`after`), so any selection of tests is runnable in any order. There
   is no suite-level `before`: anything every member needs — a plugin enabled, an identity — goes in
   each test's `given`, which the runner establishes idempotently.
2. **Every document is an implicit suite** of its own tests, addressed by its short name
   (`run markdown`), so a plugin needs no `suite` block to be runnable as a unit.
3. **Tags select suites, not tests.** `run --tag nightly` runs the union of the tests of every
   suite tagged `nightly`, de-duplicated. A test's own `tags:` are descriptive and searchable but
   do not put it in a run.

---

## `op@1.1` — the `key` field

```mdl
ext op
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1
  extends: op
  adds-fields:
    key?: NSID    # runtime operation key — what the invoker receives
```

Without `key`, a test cannot say which operation it means. The distinction is real and not
academic: `plugin-markdown` declares one `op create` but ships two runtime operations —
`org.dxos.operation.markdown.create` (agent-facing, `Database.Service`) and
`org.dxos.operation.markdown.createDraft` (the UI factory, `Capability.Service`). A test that
guesses picks the wrong one and fails at runtime.

The spec mirrors the code: one `op` block per runtime `Operation.make` key, with `key:` and
`requires:` copied from its `meta.key` and `services`. A design-level operation with no runtime
counterpart keeps its block without `key:` and says so in `status: unimplemented`, so a reader can
tell an intended operation from an existing one and a test never references it.

Populate the pre-existing `requires:` from the code's `services:` at the same time. It is
informational — see Execution Rule 1 — but it tells a reader why a step behaves as it does.

---

## Execution Rules

These are not style advice. Each was established by running a test against a live application;
a runner that ignores them produces false failures.

1. **Always invoke through the operation invoker with an explicit `spaceId`.** Do not branch on
   `requires:`. An operation's own declaration cannot see the services its *downstream* calls
   need, so `requires:` is necessary but not sufficient to predict whether a plain invoke will
   work. Taking the invoker path unconditionally removes the question. The step's `space:` names
   the space; absent, `$given.space`, else the default space.

2. **Coalesce adjacent steps that thread a live object.** The debug port serializes between
   snippets, so an ECHO object captured in one step cannot be passed to the next. Human step
   granularity and snippet granularity are different things, and reconciling them is the runner's
   job, not the author's. Prefer captures that are strings (a URI, an id, a path) and `Obj(…)` in
   the next step, which needs no coalescing at all.

3. **Verify the effect, never the return value.** A successful invocation frequently did less
   than the step intended — a `create` operation is a factory and places nothing. Judge `expect`
   against `$snapshot`, a query operation, or the DOM.

4. **Establish `given` or abort.** A test that runs against unmet preconditions reports failures
   that belong to the fixture, not the application.

5. **Assertions must be falsifiable against a dirty fixture.** An existence-shaped check
   (`some((o) => o.name === 'Notes')`) is already true when a previous run left its artifacts
   behind, so the step reports pass having done nothing. Assert on the identity the test just
   produced (`o.id === $created.object.id`), and name the test's own artifacts in `given` as
   preconditions of their absence. Artifacts carry the `QA:` prefix and `$runId` in their name.

6. **Resolve an operation by its exact key, never by suffix.** `endsWith('create')` matches both
   `org.dxos.operation.markdown.create` and `org.dxos.operation.markdown.createDraft` — the very
   ambiguity `key:` exists to remove.

7. **Three stages, run in order: `before`, `steps`, `after` — each an array of the same `step`.**
   Put a step in `before` only when it is fixture the test is not itself exercising. Creating the
   subject usually IS the test: QA-2 tests that two documents can be made and linked, so the
   creating belongs in `steps`, and `before` stays empty. `before` builds the fixture,
   `steps` is the test, `after` undoes it. The split is what makes a partial run meaningful — and
   what makes a failure legible, since a `before` failure is a broken fixture while a `steps`
   failure is a defect in the application. Every stage is a step list; `after` in particular is
   steps, not prose, and goes through operations like any other. A prose instruction ("delete the
   document") leaves each actor to pick a mechanism, and they diverge: a human uses the navtree's
   delete action, which routes through `space.removeObjects` and closes any open plank; a runner
   reaching for `space.db.remove` strands a plank pointing at an object that no longer exists,
   which the user cannot then close. `after` removes only what this run created, by identity.

8. **A partial run is the runner's option, not the test's.** A test always declares all three
   stages; which of them to run is decided per invocation. Running `before` alone stands a fixture
   up to look at; `after` alone tears one down; `steps` alone re-tests against a fixture already
   standing. Whatever is skipped must be reported — what remains and how to remove it — since the
   next full run's `given` will otherwise fail on its own leftovers (Rule 5).

9. **A step that cannot run says so in `blocked`, and the test's `status` becomes `blocked`.**
   Not `failing` — nothing was tested — and not silence, which reads as untried. A duplicate
   operation key, a missing capability or a removed operation all land here.

10. **Report by test and step number** — `QA-1.3`, or `QA-1.<id>` for a step declaring one — so a
    failure is addressable without prose. Prefer the id where a step has one: it survives a step
    being inserted ahead of it, a position does not.

11. **Snapshot after every step, and `errors` is always a finding.** The runner passes the run's
    start time as `since`, so each snapshot reports every error logged so far; a non-empty
    `errors` fails nothing by itself but is recorded against the step where it first appeared,
    even when that step's own check passed.

12. **A suite run continues past a failed test.** Each test is self-contained, so a failure in
    one — including a failed `after` — is reported and the next test's `given` guards it. A run
    stops early only when the app itself is gone: the port no longer answers, or the page has
    crashed.

---

## Example

```mdl
test QA-1: Create a space and a document, then delete the document
  actors: both
  covers: [F-1.1, T-1]
  tags: [smoke]
  given:
    - an identity exists and the default space is SPACE_READY
    - no space whose name starts with "QA:" exists — ABORT rather than deleting one this test did not create
  before:
    - name: Create a space
      do: Open the space list's +, name the space "QA: Basics $runId" and create it.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.create { name: "QA: Basics $runId" }
      capture: space
      expect: the navtree switches to the new space
      assert: return $snapshot.spaces.some((s) => s.id === $space.id && s.state === 'SPACE_READY')
  steps:
    - name: Create a document
      do: With the new space selected, click + and choose Document. Name it "QA: Doc 1".
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.markdown.create { name: "QA: Doc 1", content: "# QA: Doc 1\n" }
      space: $space.id
      capture: doc
      expect: "QA: Doc 1" appears as a row under the space
      assert: |
        const docs = await composer.invoke('org.dxos.operation.space.queryObjects',
          { typename: 'org.dxos.type.document' }, { spaceId: $space.id });
        return docs.some((o) => o.id === $doc.id);
    - name: Delete it
      do: Open the row's ⋮ menu and choose Delete.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [Obj($doc.id)] }
      space: $space.id
      expect: a "Document deleted" toast with Undo appears and the row is gone
      assert: return $snapshot.toasts.some((t) => t.title === 'Document deleted') && $snapshot.errors.length === 0
  after:
    - name: Delete the space
      do: Open the space's settings and delete the space; pick the default space.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.delete { space: Space($space.id) }
      expect: no "QA:" space remains and the workspace is the default space
      assert: return !$snapshot.spaces.some((s) => s.name?.startsWith('QA:'))
```

```mdl
suite smoke: Boots, one space, one document
  desc: The five-minute check a Routine runs on every merge to main.
  tags: [smoke, nightly]
  tests: [QA-1]
```
